For the above code blocks, each one has pre- and postconditions that can be proved by assert statements. Using the return value of the function and the original value of the list's size before and after the function call, you can express the desired properties. In general, you can use `OLD` to refer to the value of a variable at the beginning of the function and `RET` to refer to its return value.

For the array element operation, I would still have to make sure that the operation actually adds the new value and moves on to the next index. Since the problem is not very clear on all operations and their side effects, I used the most general form for each method:

<code>
    Debug.Assert(OLD(_items.Length) == _items.Length); 
    Debug.Assert(RET == _items.Length); // post-condition: size increase
    // ...

    Debug.Assert(oldCount + 1 == ArrayList.Count); // pre-condition: size increase
    Debug.Assert((int)RET < oldCount);  // return value is always a valid index
    // ...
    
    Debug.Assert(OLD(retValue) < _items.Length); // assume index is valid
    Debug.Assert(RET + 1 == ArrayList.Count); // pre-condition: size increase
    Debug.Assert((int)RET == retValue);  // return value is the inserted value
    // ...
</code>
As a side note, please consider running the provided code on a controlled environment, as debugging `Assert.Fail` with large arrays can consume a considerable amount of time and resources. Always be attentive to assertions messages when running code.--------------------
